<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mississippi-in-Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1835 CE – 1842 CE · capital Greenville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived colony on the Sinoe river in Liberia, settled by freed slaves from Mississippi plantations, which merged into Liberia in 1842.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> Colonization Society, founded by planters in the 1830s, established a colony at the mouth of the Sinoe river in 1835. Its first town was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenville-liberia">Greenville</a>. The best-known emigrants, people freed under the will of the planter Isaac Ross, could leave only after his heirs had fought the will in court for years, and reached Greenville in the late 1840s, after the colony had joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liberia">Liberia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was small and poorly supplied, run by agents of the society with settler leaders. Settlers farmed and traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kru-people">Kru people</a> of the coast, but disease, shortages and conflict with local communities made life hard, and the colony depended on help from other settlements.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1842 Mississippi-in-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, unable to survive alone, joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-liberia">Commonwealth of Liberia</a>, and it later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinoe-county">Sinoe County</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/liberia">Republic of Liberia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenville-liberia">Greenville</a> as its capital, a name the county seat still bears.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mississippi-in-africa.jpg" alt="Mississippi-in-Africa" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Augustus Mitchell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitchell_Map_Liberia_colony_1839.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1835 CE – 1842 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Greenville</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/liberia">Liberia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mississippi in Africa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=mississippi-in-africa" aria-label="Mississippi-in-Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4430 1990 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mississippi-in-Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4750 2246L4701 2198"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4701 2198h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4750 2246h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4750 2246h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Greenville. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=mississippi-in-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
